A||||19||FIEROCIOUS||Hood scoops and fun!||||6||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||02-07-2000||02:00 AM||||I'm looking for some ideas to spice up my Fiero and give ideas to others as well!<BR>Such as a 79-83 mustang scoop on the rear deck, Trans Am nose vents mounted upside down to extract air from the radiator, or even the older T/A's side air extractors grafted on the front. <BR>Rules: 1. It has to look good.<BR>       2. It has to be functional.<BR>  (such as heat removal or aerodynamics)<BR>       3. Rate the difficulty.<BR>  (bolt on, bond on or complete overhaul)<BR>Anyone thought about the new touring style wing as an alternative to the factory wing?||||reg||6
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-07-2000||04:51 AM||||That last casual part about the wing.<BR>I have the great fortune to live in an area that gets down to 65 degrees below at times.  Aair holds zero moisture at about 40 below and colder.  Point is the exhaust water condenses immediately, givings graphic picture of rear airflow.  Most vehicles have large spirals spinning off each corner, three cars wide and two cars high.  Short tall cars and pickups actually have air going forward over the vehicle.  Moving air takes horsepower, the larger the amount of air the more horsepower.  Porsche's have a tiny ball of smooth air riding so close and sharp to the rear deck its almost part of it.  Fiero's with the factory wing have that ball of air riding about four inches back, no vortex, and fluctuating up and down about two inches, right between wing and deck.  'No wing' Fiero's don't have a vortex either but the boundary is more turbulent, up and down about a foot.  Functionally the factory wing is doing exactly what it should, straightening the air to get a sharp cutoff on the turbulence, saving horsepower to go fast.  Less than meausurable at street speeds.  Mostly just wanted to show off that cold isn't all bad.  <p>[This message has been edited by Phaeton (edited 02-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||02-07-2000||05:40 AM||||Tom sells a version of the Mustang scoop for the rear deck.  It's functional (pulls hot air out) and fairly inexpensive.  Advertised as bolt-on (I think), but would look much better molded in IMHO.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||02-07-2000||11:00 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Scoop1.jpg"> <BR>Here's my functional scoop. I have it bolted on and use a Grand Prix air cleaner system to duct the air. I now use the side scoop for engine compartment cooling. Indy style scoop is $200 and to have it painted (gel-coated) was $150. I use a stainless steel screen to help keep out debris.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-07-2000||11:02 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hood2.jpg"> <BR>Also Here is an pic of a friends hood. I was thinking about having it done also. The whole job was around $500. What does everyone think of this style?||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIEROCIOUS||02-07-2000||01:45 PM||||Great pics guys! Is their a phone number or E-mail adress for the mustang scoop? also were did the nifty blue hood come from?<BR>Has anyone tried the side thingys from 81-83 Cameros? they look flat and I have seen them as scoops for the air inlet on the side (looks real good too! better if molded in though!) I am currently useing the quarter window scoop on my 87 GT it does help to pull air into the void of air behind the window and helps cooling! Bad blind spot, but I don't belive in blind spots I belive in bad drivers! The one with the window would help.<P>Aerodynamics: I've always asumed that the less scoops and spoilers you have the (slicker) you are. Nacta ducts are of the slickest like the front of the viper. I love the import boys with the three foot tall quadruple wings that stick out a foot each side and require 500 hp just to move! LOL!||||reg||14
